  "provider_query": "RFC 791 text for the Internet Protocol Version field",
  "answer": "**RFC 791 (Internet Protocol, September 1981) — Version field**\n\nFrom the header format specification (section 3.1):\n\n> **Version: 4 bits**  \n> The Version field indicates the format of the internet header. This document describes version 4.\n\nIt is the first field in the internet header diagram (`|Version| IHL |…`). The glossary repeats: “The Version field indicates the format of the internet header.”\n\n### Relevant results (concise sourcing)\n1. **RFC Editor STD 5 / RFC 791 text** — Exact wording above, with the header diagram. https://www.rfc-editor.org/std/std5.txt  \n2. **RFC Editor info page for RFC 791** — STD 5, Internet Standard, J. Postel, September 1981; obsoletes RFC 760; updated by RFC 1349, RFC 2474, RFC 6864. https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc791  \n3. **Debian doc-rfc copy of rfc791.txt** — Glossary entry: “Version — The Version field indicates the format of the internet header.” https://sources.debian.org/src/doc-rfc/20120225-2/rfc791.txt  \n4. **PTAB exhibit excerpt of RFC 791** — Same header diagram and “Version: 4 bits / … describes version 4.”  \n5. **IANA IP Version Numbers** — Notes the IP version field is 4 bits (citing RFC 791 and RFC 8200). Decimal 4 = Internet Protocol (RFC 791); 6 = IPv6 (RFC 8200). https://www.iana.org/assignments/version-numbers/version-numbers.xhtml  \n6. **IANA version-numbers.txt** — Same 4-bit field note and registry (value 4 = IP / RFC 791). https://www.iana.org/assignments/version-numbers/version-numbers.txt  \n7. **Wikipedia IPv4** — Summarizes the first header field as Version (4 bits), always 4 for IPv4, per RFC 791. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPv4  \n8. **freeCodeCamp IPv4 handbook** — Describes the 4-bit Version field from RFC 791 as carrying value 4 so the rest of the header is interpreted as IPv4. https://www.freecodecamp.org/news/how-ipv4-works-a-handbook-for-developers/",
